      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 2,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "Meeting Title\n-------------\n\nData Dictionary Review & Consent Management Requirements Lock-down\n\nDate and Time\n-------------\n\nJuly 13, 2026 14:30-15:30\n\nAttendees\n---------\n\n| Name | Role / Affiliation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Tim | ACN Architect |\n| Andrew | TNL – Martech / Analytics Lead |\n| Vicky | Zimmermann – DIGITAL PERFORMANCE & OPTMISATION SENIOR MANAGER |\n| Angelo |  |\n| Jasmijn |  |\n| Bianca |  |\n|  |  |\n\n---\n\nAgenda Items\n------------\n\n### 1. Data Dictionary Review\n\nThe data dictionary was walked through at a high level. It is structured across several tabs:\n\n* **Main / Objectives tab** – outlines the primary requirements for tracking.\n* **GA4 Events Listing tab** – lists current GA4 events.\n* **Data Layer tab** – a static view of what the current data layer looks like.\n* **Events tab** – the most relevant tab in the context of re-platforming; it covers the creation of new events and the removal of redundant ones.\n\n**Key discussion points:**\n\n* **Data layer ownership:** Tim raised the question of whether TNL is responsible for designing the new data layer. This remains a grey area. TNL created the structure of the *current* data layer and has suggestions, but the design and build of the *new* data layer is not clearly scoped to them. Tim confirmed TNL does not have scope to build the data layer from scratch; they will build to a defined spec, mapping clicks to events (e.g. \"add to cart\") and handling the integration to push that data layer to GTM / Addingwell.\n* **TNL build scope clarification:** For TNL to create Google Tag Manager tags and triggers, Tim needs a spec for page load events and other events. TNL will build an analytics utility class that grabs the spec, passes the properties, and sends them to GTM. TNL will build the integration, but they require the spec first.\n* **Offline follow-up:** Data layer design is within TNL's skillset. Vicky and Andrew agreed to catch up offline to align on this.\n* **Martech tagging philosophy (Andrew):** All martech tags should be centralised in GTM as much as possible. Tags and snippets should not be stored in the backend. The overall design is performance-oriented, with tracking running server-side and very little client-side.\n* **Data dictionary on Confluence:** Tim requested that the final data dictionary be published to Confluence so that events can be more easily tagged to the appropriate user stories.\n* **Timeline for data layer design:** Vicky asked when the data layer design is needed. Tim indicated it is needed as early as possible — TNL needs it done before they can develop the analytics utility class (which takes time to write, covering the different utilities callable based on events). As part of component development, TNL will build to the spec and write tests around it. This typically needs to be in place within the first month of build. Andrew noted it should be easy to change and lightweight — it acts as the bridge between backend and frontend. It is an early input but also an ongoing concern that grows and is iterated on as the project progresses. Tim confirmed that iteration over time is expected.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Consent Management – Requirements Lock-down\n\n*(This agenda item was scheduled for discussion — please add notes if covered in the meeting.)*\n\n* Goal: lock down requirements for consent-managed items.\n* This is postponed - consent review is in progress with TNL / Zimmermann\n\n---\n\nAction Items\n------------\n\n| Task | Owner | Due Date | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Publish final Data Dictionary to Confluence | Andrew / Vicky | TBC | So Tim's team can link events to user stories |\n| Define and share data layer spec (page load events + other events) | Andrew / Vicky | As early as possible – target first month of build | TNL are blocked on their utility class until this is received |\n| Vicky & Andrew catch up offline on data layer design ownership | Vicky, Andrew | TBC | Clarify what TNL vs Zimmermann will own in the new data layer design |\n| Lock down list of tools/tags requiring Didomi Consent | TBC | TBC | Consent management requirements to be finalised |\n\n---\n\nNext Steps\n----------\n\n* Vicky and Andrew to align offline on data layer design responsibilities.\n* Final data dictionary to be uploaded to Confluence for TNL's reference.\n* Data layer spec to be delivered to TNL as early as possible to unblock utility class development.\n* Consent-managed items list (Didomi) to be finalised and documented.\n\n---\n\nOther Notes\n-----------\n\n* The data layer spec is expected to evolve throughout the project — not a one-time deliverable.\n* TNL will not build the data layer itself, but will build the integration layer that pushes the data layer contents to GTM or Addingwell.\n* There are approximately 150 rows of events in the current data dictionary; a significant number will need to be removed as part of re-platforming.",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f4dd",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

